---
// versioning, and CHANGELOG.md from the repository root, rendered as it is.
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Term from '../../components/Term.astro';
import { Content as Changelog, getHeadings } from '../../../../CHANGELOG.md';
import { url } from '../../data/site';

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'versioning', text: 'versioning' },
  ...getHeadings(),
];
---
<Doc
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'versioning & changelog',
    description: 'What a version number of the nalet design system promises, and what changed in every release.',
  }}
  headings={headings}
>
  <h1>versioning & changelog</h1>
  <p>What a version number promises, and the list of what changed in every release so far.</p>
  <h2 id="versioning">versioning</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Version numbers have three parts</strong>, following <Term id="semantic-versioning">semantic versioning</Term>.
      While the system is below 1.0, a new middle number, such as 0.3.0, may change how existing components look, and the
      changelog lists every change you can see. A new last number, such as 0.3.1, only fixes things.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One release per version.</strong> Tagging a version, such as v0.3.1, builds the library and attaches the
      packed package to a release on GitHub. Apps install it from that address; see <a href={url('develop/react/')}>React</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>This site follows the latest code.</strong> It is rebuilt from the main branch after every change, so it can
      describe changes that aren't in a release yet. The version in its code samples is the latest release.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Below is the <Term id="changelog">changelog</Term> file from the repository, shown as it is.</p>
  <Changelog />
</Doc>
